# A Java .gitignore that is actually right.
#
# The rule behind every line: ignore what a BUILD produces, never what a build NEEDS.
# Both halves matter. The first half keeps a repository readable. The second half is the
# one people get wrong, and it breaks the build for everybody except the person who wrote
# the rule - because their working copy still has the file the clone does not.

# ---- what the build produces ------------------------------------------------
target/            # Maven
build/             # Gradle
out/               # IntelliJ's own output directory
bin/               # Eclipse's
*.class            # in case anything compiles outside those

# ---- what the tools leave behind --------------------------------------------
.gradle/           # Gradle's per-project cache. NOT the same thing as gradle/
*.log
*.hprof            # heap dumps
*.jfr              # flight recordings
.DS_Store          # macOS
.idea/             # IntelliJ project metadata
*.iml

# ---- KEEP, and these are the ones a careless rule swallows -------------------
# gradlew and gradlew.bat            - the wrapper scripts. Executable, and tracked.
# gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar  - 47 KB of BINARY that gradlew runs. A `*.jar`
#                                      rule ignores it, `git status` then says clean,
#                                      and the next person to clone gets a gradlew that
#                                      cannot start.
# gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.properties - the line that pins the Gradle version.
# .mvn/                              - Maven's per-project configuration: jvm.config,
#                                      maven.config, and the Maven wrapper if you use one.
#
# There is no rule to write for these. The rule is: do not write a `*.jar` rule.
# If you must have one, exclude the wrapper explicitly, in this order:
#
#     *.jar
#     !gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar
#
# A `!` line only works if no parent DIRECTORY is ignored - git never looks inside an
# ignored directory, so `gradle/` followed by `!gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar`
# does nothing at all.
